\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The two commutants in rank 4}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Over #{\mathbb{R}}, the algebra #{\operatorname{Cl}(3,0)}
is isomorphic to #{M_2(\mathbb{C})}, and the commutant of its
simple module is #{\mathbb{C}}. The algebra
#{\operatorname{Cl}(0,3)} is isomorphic to
#{\mathbb{H}\oplus\mathbb{H}}, and the commutant of either
simple module is #{\mathbb{H}}. These are two of the three
endomorphism rings in \ref{fdip-0051}.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{e_1,e_2,e_3} be an orthonormal basis of a positive
three-dimensional space, so #{e_i^2=1} and the distinct
generators anticommute. The Pauli matrices
##{\sigma_1=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\ 
 \sigma_2=\begin{pmatrix}0&-\ui\\\ui&0\end{pmatrix},\ 
 \sigma_3=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}}
satisfy the same squares and anticommutators, and
#{\sigma_1\sigma_2\sigma_3=\ui\bbI}. The resulting homomorphism
sends the volume element to #{\ui\bbI}. The 8 matrices
#{\bbI,\sigma_k,\ui\bbI,\ui\sigma_k} are a real basis of
#{M_2(\mathbb{C})}, so the map is an isomorphism. Scalar
matrices are the commutant, and they are the copy of
#{\mathbb{C}} spanned by #{\bbI} and #{\ui\bbI}.}

\p{For #{\operatorname{Cl}(0,3)} let #{f_i^2=-1}, with
distinct generators anticommuting, and set
#{\omega=f_1f_2f_3}. Passing #{f_1} through #{f_2} and
#{f_3} produces two signs, so #{\omega f_1=f_1\omega}, and
likewise for the other generators. Then
##{\omega^2=f_1f_2f_3f_1f_2f_3
 =-f_2f_3f_2f_3=1,}
where the first equality uses #{f_3f_1=-f_1f_3},
#{f_2f_1=-f_1f_2}, and #{f_1^2=-1}, and the second uses
#{f_3f_2=-f_2f_3} together with #{f_i^2=-1}.
The central idempotents #{(1\pm\omega)/2} split the algebra.
On the factor #{\omega=1}, one has #{f_3=-f_1f_2}, and
#{(f_1f_2)^2=-1}. Thus #{f_1}, #{f_2} and #{f_1f_2} obey the
quaternion relations, so that factor is a copy of
#{\mathbb{H}}. The factor #{\omega=-1} is the second copy.
Each simple module is the regular module of
#{\mathbb{H}}, whose commutant is right multiplication by
#{\mathbb{H}}.}
}
